    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> decided in 1505 to demolish the fourth-century basilica of Constantine and replace it with a monumental church, and a succession of popes and architects took about 120 years to finish the work.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donato-bramante">Bramante</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, Peruzzi, Sangallo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> and Maderno each changed the plan, and Michelangelo is regarded as the principal designer of the large part of the building that stands today.</li><li>Catholic tradition holds that Saint Peter is buried directly below the high altar, and excavations begun in 1939 found shrines built over an aedicula holding bones, though it could not be determined with certainty that they were Peter's.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>To help pay for the rebuilding, indulgences were granted in return for contributions, and the salesmanship of the preacher Johann Tetzel, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-brandenburg">Albrecht of Mainz</a>, provoked a scandal that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

